I don't think that going to church is manifestly an action predicated on 
accepting the Bible as largely true.  It is manifestly an action 
predicated on accepting that teachers of religion should be obeyed.
I mean anyone in his good mind can try and find his own interpretations 
of the Bible or at least realize that the interpretations given in 
sermons have a large degree of ... arbitrariness.  The Bible is a book 
with many authors, a fact which has given it a huge amount of 
interpretability, because it contains self-contradictions and tries to 
reconcile not only opposing views but even different accounts of 
history.  You can either take the Bible literally or seriously but not 
both.  Many  preachers from the pulpit do not seem to be aware of this. 
  IMO, the Bible contains many parts worth reading and even wisdom.  It 
also contains stupidity.  Nonetheless, I will not become a church-goer, 
just because I think the Bible is true in some places or even if I 
thought that it is largely true - as a historic account, it may in fact 
be...

So my remark was not intended to kick off a discussion on the Bible but 
I just wanted to emphasize that believing in the Bible and being a 
church-goer are two separate things.  I am willing to admit that the set 
of church-goers contains a subset of Bible-believers, but neither are 
all church-goers Bible-believers nor all Bible-believers church-goers.


